mptcp-bpf-dscp for Debian (server/VPS side)
=============================================

This is the server/VPS counterpart of the router's OpenWrt
mptcp-bpf-dscp + mptcp-dscp-manager packages built by the same
openmptcprouter-feeds source tree (see that repo's ARCHITECTURE.md for
the router-vs-VPS split, and src/mptcp_bpf_dscp.c's header comment for
the two pinning modes this scheduler supports).

On the router, each WAN has its own local IP, so DSCP classes are
pinned to a WAN via that IP (dscp_iface map). On this VPS, every
subflow arrives on the same local IP, so pin by MPTCP remote endpoint
id instead (dscp_remote_id map):

    mptcp-scheduler-dscp.sh set <dscp> id <N>

<N> is the MPTCP endpoint id the router assigned to the WAN that
should carry <dscp> traffic. On the router that id is
network.<iface>.ip4table, kept stable across reconnects (DHCP renewal,
mobile reconnects) once the router's mptcp/multipath script passes it
explicitly to 'ip mptcp endpoint add ... id <N>' instead of letting
the kernel auto-assign one. Look it up on the router with:

    uci get network.<iface>.ip4table

There is currently no automated sync of this id from the router to
the VPS - copy the number over manually (or script it via whatever
already talks to both ends, e.g. the openmptcprouter-api).

    mptcp-scheduler-dscp.sh show

lists every currently configured pin, both by interface/IP and by
remote id.

Service
-------

mptcp-bpf-dscp.service loads /usr/share/bpf/scheduler/mptcp_bpf_dscp.o, pins it
(and its maps) under /sys/fs/bpf/mptcp, and sets it as
net.mptcp.scheduler at boot. This changes the *system-wide* default
MPTCP scheduler on this host - only install this package on a VPS
dedicated to terminating OpenMPTCProuter multipath connections.

After building/installing a new mptcp_bpf_dscp.o (e.g. after editing
the scheduler), run:

    systemctl restart mptcp-bpf-dscp

Stopping the service switches net.mptcp.scheduler back to "default"
before unpinning, so it doesn't leave the sysctl pointed at a
scheduler name the kernel no longer has registered.

Requirements
------------

A kernel with MPTCP struct_ops scheduler support (BPF_STRUCT_OPS for
mptcp_sched_ops), CONFIG_BPF, CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF and CONFIG_MPTCP
enabled. This is a fairly recent kernel feature - check
'bpftool struct_ops register' actually succeeds if the service fails
to start (see 'systemctl status mptcp-bpf-dscp').
